Z||000000||Seanh||08-17-2001||10:46 PM||||I am so sick and tired of my constant trouble with my steering column I feel as if I should find someone I don't care for and beat them over the head with my column to take out some anger.....<P>I just stripped down my old column (it had a huge dead spot from an accident) because I got my other one in the mail today. Well, I got it all stripped down and started putting things on the replacement column when I decided to try turning it. It is almost as difficult to turn as my old one! Also, the shaft had some play, like the bearings are worn out. I'm getting so sick of screwing around for weeks on end with this damn steering column, I'v had enough already! Anyway, my old column really turns easy, except the massive dead spot and where it catches. I am seriously considering removing the shaft from inside the black caseing and fixing my old column again..What do you think? I'd like to know how much of a *****  it is to get it outta there, and how to go about it. I usally just find screws and start unscrewing, so I'm gonna try that first right now. Also, I'd like to know where I can find parts for this column, obviously I'm going to need somthing because its got a problem. So, what should I do? I can get another column from a salvage yard here for $125. One from Kick-hill is $100. I only paid $40 for mine, so I thought I got a heck of a deal, but its seems to have about the same amount of trouble mine does....BTW, this is a column for an automatic.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 4spd Auto (Currently in repair, wrecked by "Red-Headed Step Child")<BR><I>I live my life a 1/4 mile at a time. Nothing else matters</I>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Jim Jimenez||08-18-2001||12:07 AM||||Sounds as if your biggest problem is in the rack, and not the column if you have a "dead spot" The column itself is usually a fairly easy fix and can be done in the car. If the whole top of the column seems loose, it is the four anchor bolts. To tighten them, you have to have a lock plate remover and a pivot pin puller. The bolts are located under the tilt housing. If only the upper shaft is loose or noisy, It may be the upper bearings at fault, Get kit #7844651<BR>from your GM dealer which includes a new sector and rack as well as the bearings. There is no substitute for a GM shop manual is you are not sure what you are doing. ||||reg||10
